\title{Cantor, Aleph, Beth, and Hartogs:\\A Self-Contained Account of Successive Infinite Sizes}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 21, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a compact account of fundamental mechanisms for comparing infinite sizes. Starting from cardinal comparison by injections and bijections, we prove Cantor's theorem through the diagonal subset and deduce that every power set is strictly larger than its underlying set. We present the initial aleph and beth numbers: $\aleph_0$ is the cardinality of the natural numbers, $\aleph_1$ is its successor cardinal, and every successor step of the beth hierarchy is strict. The first beth number is $2^{\aleph_0}$, the cardinality $\cont$ of the continuum. This gives $\aleph_1\leq\cont$ and identifies the continuum hypothesis with $\beth_1=\aleph_1$. Finally, a Hartogs successor object $H(X)$ of cardinality $|X|^+$ is shown to admit an embedding of $X$ while admitting no embedding into $X$. Algorithms and finite models illustrate diagonalization, power-set growth, and successor growth. The account distinguishes enumeration, passage to all subsets, and passage to the least strictly larger cardinal.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Cardinality extends finite counting by declaring two sets to have the same size when their elements admit a bijection. Its consequences for infinite sets differ sharply from finite intuition. The natural numbers and their even members have the same cardinality, because $n\mapsto2n$ is a bijection. Containment therefore does not imply strict cardinal inequality for infinite sets.

Strict growth nevertheless occurs in robust ways. The first is the power-set operation: for every set $X$, the set $\Pow(X)$ of all subsets of $X$ is strictly larger than $X$. The second is successor-cardinal formation: for a cardinal $\kappa$, its successor $\kappa^+$ is the least cardinal strictly larger than $\kappa$. These operations generate the beth and aleph viewpoints respectively.

They first meet at the continuum. The size of $\N$ is $\aleph_0$. The size of its power set is $2^{\aleph_0}=\beth_1$, which is also the size $\cont$ of the real line. Meanwhile $\aleph_1=\aleph_0^+$ is the least uncountable cardinal. Cantor's theorem ensures $\aleph_1\leq\cont$, while equality is the continuum hypothesis.

A third construction, associated with Hartogs, expresses escape from a set through well-ordering. A Hartogs successor object $H(X)$ has cardinality $|X|^+$. The strict inequality gives $X\hookrightarrow H(X)$ and rules out $H(X)\hookrightarrow X$. The classical Hartogs theorem additionally emphasizes that an ordinal not injectible into $X$ can be obtained without a global choice principle; this foundational refinement is discussed below.

\section{Cardinal comparison and power sets}
\begin{definition}
A function $f:X\to Y$ is injective if $f(x)=f(x')$ implies $x=x'$, surjective if every $y\in Y$ equals $f(x)$ for some $x\in X$, and bijective if both conditions hold. We write $|X|=|Y|$ when a bijection exists, $|X|\leq|Y|$ when an injection exists, and $|X|<|Y|$ when $|X|\leq|Y|$ but equality fails.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
The power set of $X$ is $\Pow(X)=\{A:A\subseteq X\}$. Identifying a subset with its characteristic function $X\to\{0,1\}$ gives $|\Pow(X)|=2^{|X|}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Cantor's diagonal theorem]
For every set $X$ and every function $f:X\to\Pow(X)$, the function $f$ is not surjective.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Define
\[
D_f=\{x\in X:x\notin f(x)\}.
\]
If $f$ were surjective, then $f(a)=D_f$ for some $a\in X$. But then
\[
a\in D_f\iff a\notin f(a)\iff a\notin D_f,
\]
a contradiction. Thus $D_f$ is omitted from the range.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No equivalence with a power set]
No bijection $X\to\Pow(X)$ exists.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
A bijection would be surjective, contrary to Cantor's diagonal theorem.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict power-set growth]
For every set $X$, $|X|<|\Pow(X)|$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The map $x\mapsto\{x\}$ is injective, so $|X|\leq|\Pow(X)|$. Equality is excluded by the preceding corollary.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No largest cardinal]
For every cardinal $\kappa$, some cardinal is strictly greater than $\kappa$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Choose $X$ with $|X|=\kappa$ and use $|\Pow(X)|=2^\kappa>\kappa$.
\end{proof}

The diagonal proof is stronger than a numerical estimate: from any alleged complete list, it explicitly manufactures an omitted subset. It also applies unchanged to finite sets, although elementary counting then supplies an alternative proof.

\section{Countability and the first alephs}
\begin{definition}
A set is countably infinite when it is equinumerous with $\N$. The cardinality of $\N$ is denoted $\aleph_0$. The successor cardinal $\kappa^+$ is the least cardinal strictly greater than $\kappa$.
\end{definition}

The aleph hierarchy enumerates infinite well-ordered cardinalities. Its initial terms obey
\[
\aleph_0=|\N|,\qquad \aleph_1=\aleph_0^+.
\]

\begin{theorem}[Initial aleph growth]
The first two alephs satisfy $\aleph_0<\aleph_1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By definition, every cardinal is strictly below its successor, and $\aleph_1=\aleph_0^+$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Successor characterization]
The identity $\aleph_1=\aleph_0^+$ holds.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
At a successor index, the aleph hierarchy takes the successor cardinal. Applying this at the successor of the initial index gives the statement.
\end{proof}

Consequently, if $\lambda$ is any cardinal with $\aleph_0<\lambda$, then $\aleph_1\leq\lambda$. This is the least-uncountable characterization of $\aleph_1$.

\subsection{Why familiar enlargements remain countable}
Several examples clarify why a dedicated successor cardinal is needed. The integers remain countable: list them as $0,1,-1,2,-2,\ldots$. The rationals remain countable as well. Arrange fractions by increasing $|p|+q$, where $p\in\mathbb Z$ and $q\in\N$ is positive, and skip repeated values caused by unreduced representations. Every rational eventually appears.

Finite strings over a finite or countable alphabet also form a countable set. Over a finite alphabet, order strings by length and then lexicographically. Over a countable alphabet, encode a finite string as a finite tuple of natural numbers and encode tuples by repeated pairing. Thus adding finite descriptions or countably many countable layers need not pass beyond $\aleph_0$.

In contrast, infinite binary sequences correspond bijectively to subsets of $\N$: the sequence $(b_n)$ represents $\{n:b_n=1\}$. Cantor's theorem gives
\[
\aleph_0<2^{\aleph_0}.
\]
Arbitrary finite constructions over countable data often preserve countability, whereas an independent binary choice at every natural-number position creates the full power set. The least possible larger cardinal is $\aleph_1$; the binary-sequence space has size $\beth_1=\cont$. Their possible equality is the continuum question.

\section{The beth hierarchy}
\begin{definition}
At initial and successor stages, the beth hierarchy is defined by
\[
\beth_0=\aleph_0,\qquad \beth_{\alpha+1}=2^{\beth_\alpha}.
\]
At limit ordinals the hierarchy takes the supremum of prior values.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Strict beth successor step]
For every ordinal $\alpha$, $\beth_\alpha<\beth_{\alpha+1}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Choose $X$ with $|X|=\beth_\alpha$. Then
\[
\beth_{\alpha+1}=2^{\beth_\alpha}=|\Pow(X)|>|X|=\beth_\alpha
\]
by strict power-set growth.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The first beth number]
The first beth number satisfies $\beth_1=2^{\aleph_0}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Set $\alpha=0$ in the successor definition and use $\beth_0=\aleph_0$.
\end{proof}

The aleph successor asks for the least next cardinal, whereas the beth successor applies a specified operation. Cantor proves the latter is larger but does not prove it is the immediate successor.

\section{The continuum hypothesis}
\begin{definition}
The continuum cardinal is $\cont=|\R|$. The continuum hypothesis, abbreviated CH, is the assertion $\cont=\aleph_1$.
\end{definition}

The standard binary-sequence comparison gives
\[
\cont=2^{\aleph_0}.
\]
Every subset of $\N$ determines a binary sequence. Such sequences embed into a Cantor-type subset of $[0,1]$. Conversely, real numbers have countable descriptions through nested rational intervals or digit sequences. The ambiguity of the two expansions possessed by some reals does not alter the cardinal comparison. Hence $\cont=\beth_1$.

\begin{theorem}[Unconditional continuum bound]
One has $\aleph_1\leq\cont$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Cantor's theorem gives $\aleph_0<2^{\aleph_0}=\cont$. Since $\aleph_1$ is the least cardinal above $\aleph_0$, it follows that $\aleph_1\leq\cont$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Beth formulation of CH]
The continuum hypothesis holds if and only if $\beth_1=\aleph_1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Substitute the identity $\cont=\beth_1$ into the definition $\cont=\aleph_1$.
\end{proof}

The theorem is an equivalence of formulations, not a proof of CH. Cantor supplies strict growth from $\aleph_0$ to $2^{\aleph_0}$; it does not determine how many successor-cardinal steps that jump crosses.

\section{A Hartogs successor object}
\begin{definition}[Hartogs successor object]
For a set $X$, a Hartogs successor object $H(X)$ is a well-ordered set with
\[
|H(X)|=|X|^+.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Cardinality]
For every $X$, the Hartogs successor object satisfies $|H(X)|=|X|^+$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
This is the defining choice of $H(X)$: take the canonical well-ordered representative of the successor cardinal.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Hartogs non-embedding]
There is no injection $H(X)\hookrightarrow X$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Such an injection would imply $|H(X)|\leq|X|$. Since $|H(X)|=|X|^+$ and $|X|<|X|^+$, this is impossible.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Forward embedding]
There is an injection $X\hookrightarrow H(X)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The strict inequality $|X|<|X|^+=|H(X)|$ entails the required injection by the definition of cardinal comparison.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Strict Hartogs growth]
For every $X$, $|X|<|H(X)|$.
\end{corollary}

The objects $H(X)$ and $\Pow(X)$ both exceed $X$, but encode different targets. The first has exactly the next cardinality; the second has cardinality $2^{|X|}$ and may be larger than the immediate successor.

\subsection{The classical foundational statement}
Classical Hartogs' theorem says that for every set $X$ there exists an ordinal $h(X)$ for which no injection $h(X)\to X$ exists. It is constructed from order types of well-orderings of subsets of $X$, without first assuming a global principle that every set is well-orderable. The successor-cardinal presentation isolates the same non-embedding pattern in a framework where the cardinal successor and a well-ordered representative are already available. A choice-free axiomatic account should use the direct construction from well-ordered subsets.

\section{Algorithms and finite models}
Finite computations cannot settle infinite-cardinal questions, but they display the mechanisms exactly.

\subsection{Diagonal escape}
Given an $n\times n$ Boolean matrix $M$, regard row $i$ as the characteristic vector of $f(i)$. Define
\[
d_i=1-M_{ii}.
\]
For every $i$, the output $d$ differs from row $i$ at coordinate $i$, hence is absent from the list. Reading and negating the diagonal takes $O(n)$ time and $O(n)$ output space. No off-diagonal entry need be inspected. This is the finite combinatorial pattern of Cantor's proof.

\subsection{Power-set enumeration}
For $X=\{0,\ldots,n-1\}$, identify a subset with an $n$-bit mask. Enumerating masks $0$ through $2^n-1$ lists every subset exactly once. Directly outputting all membership vectors costs $\Theta(n2^n)$ bit operations and $O(2^n)$ subset records, which is optimal up to representation constants because that much output is produced.

\subsection{Finite successor witness}
Let $X_n=\{0,\ldots,n-1\}$ and $H_n=\{0,\ldots,n\}$. Inclusion embeds $X_n$ into $H_n$, while the pigeonhole principle forbids an injection $H_n\to X_n$. This models the directionality of the Hartogs successor theorem. It does not claim that adjoining one point creates an infinite successor cardinal: for infinite $X$, adjoining finitely many points leaves cardinality unchanged.

\section{Applications and conceptual bridges}
\subsection{Predicates and classifiers}
Subsets $A\subseteq X$ correspond bijectively to Boolean predicates $X\to\{0,1\}$. Thus the unrestricted class of binary classifiers on $X$ has cardinality
\[
2^{|X|}>|X|.
\]
No indexing by the input points can list every classifier. This is a structural counting statement, not a statistical-learning claim; practical learnability depends on restricted hypothesis classes, distributions, capacity, and sample access.

\subsection{Languages and bit streams}
Finite words over a countable alphabet form a countable set. A formal language is a subset of that set, so the collection of all languages has size $2^{\aleph_0}=\cont$. The same pattern applies to infinite binary streams and arbitrary yes-or-no properties of natural numbers. Consequently, countably many finite descriptions cannot name all such objects.

\subsection{Diagonalization as adversarial construction}
The diagonal subset is relative to the proposed enumeration. It changes the answer at index $x$ from the answer made by $f(x)$. Its force comes from disciplined self-reference: $x$ is an element, $f(x)$ is a subset, and the proposition $x\in f(x)$ can be negated. Similar adversarial diagonal constructions recur in impossibility and non-enumerability arguments.

\subsection{Two meanings of ``next''}
The hierarchies answer different questions. The cardinal $\aleph_{\alpha+1}$ is the least cardinal above $\aleph_\alpha$. The cardinal $\beth_{\alpha+1}$ is the size of all subsets of a set of size $\beth_\alpha$. Cantor guarantees every beth successor is a genuine increase, but not an immediate one. At the first stage, CH is precisely the claim that the two notions coincide.

\section{Scope and limitations}
Several boundaries are essential. First, CH has been defined and reformulated, not proved. The unconditional result is $\aleph_1\leq\cont$. Replacing ``strictly larger'' with ``immediate successor'' would be invalid.

Second, strictness at beth successor stages follows from Cantor, while a full transfinite analysis also needs limit stages. At a limit $\lambda$, one sets $\beth_\lambda=\sup_{\alpha<\lambda}\beth_\alpha$; comparison there involves cofinality and cannot be reduced to one diagonal step.

Third, classical Hartogs theory has a foundational role beyond the successor-object presentation. A direct construction of a non-embeddable ordinal works without assuming $X$ is well-orderable. Global cardinal comparison can carry additional background assumptions. The non-embedding argument here is valid once $H(X)$ is specified with cardinality $|X|^+$, while a fully choice-free development should construct the Hartogs ordinal from well-ordered subsets.

Fourth, inaccessible, measurable, strongly compact, and supercompact cardinals require additional structure: closure properties, filters, ultrafilters, elementary embeddings, and universe bookkeeping. Relative consistency is a metamathematical relation among theories and models; it cannot be inferred merely from definitions.

\section{Future work}
Natural continuations are:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item Construct Hartogs' ordinal in explicit axiomatic set theory and prove non-embedding without global choice.
\item Derive the least-uncountable characterization of $\aleph_1$ through countability predicates and connect it to $\aleph_0^+$.
\item Establish equivalent forms of CH, including the absence of a cardinal strictly between $\aleph_0$ and $\cont$ and the dichotomy for infinite subsets of $\R$.
\item Extend the beth hierarchy through limit stages and compare the complete aleph and beth hierarchies.
\item Develop the infrastructure for large-cardinal definitions and formulate consistency-strength claims only in an explicit metatheory of models.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}
Diagonalization turns any proposed enumeration $X\to\Pow(X)$ into a subset it misses, proving $|X|<2^{|X|}$. Successor-cardinal formation identifies $\aleph_1$ as $\aleph_0^+$ and supplies a Hartogs successor object $H(X)$ with $X\hookrightarrow H(X)$ but no injection in reverse. The beth hierarchy iterates power-set growth, yielding $\beth_1=2^{\aleph_0}=\cont$ and strict growth at every successor stage.

Together these facts give
\[
\aleph_0<\aleph_1\leq\cont=\beth_1,
\]
while CH is exactly the assertion that the middle inequality is equality. The hierarchy of infinity is generated by explicit operations and constrained by rigorous inequalities, yet elementary cardinal arguments alone do not determine the exact gap between countability and the continuum.

\end{document}

