<details><summary>Reveal the hierarchy statements</summary>
The aleph hierarchy begins with $\aleph_0=|\mathbb N|$ and $\aleph_1=\aleph_0^+$. The beth hierarchy begins with $\beth_0=\aleph_0$ and $\beth_{\alpha+1}=2^{\beth_\alpha}$. Hence every beth successor is strict, $\beth_1=2^{\aleph_0}$, and $\aleph_1\leq\beth_1$. Since the continuum has size $2^{\aleph_0}$, CH is equivalent to $\beth_1=\aleph_1$.
</details>

## 4. Escape by a successor object

For any $X$, choose a well-ordered object $H(X)$ of size $|X|^+$. Then $X$ injects into $H(X)$, but no injection returns from $H(X)$ to $X$.

